sync, leaving about 82ms for the Merkle tree span—which again includes all of segment 1's RS encoding plus the actual tree construction. The Merkle tree code synchronizes when it reads back the root hash, forcing all prior kernels to complete. So the total GPU work for `stacked_commit` breaks down to roughly 10ms for stack traces and about 30ms for RS encoding.\n\nThe remaining GPU time goes to NTT, interpolation, bit reversal, and the Merkle tree itself—row hashing plus tree layers—totaling around 100-130ms per segment. The wall-clock times of 165ms and 87ms are larger because they include CPU overhead and synchronization costs. But segment 1's 87ms is puzzling if both segments process similar AIR counts; the difference likely comes down to segment 1 having smaller trace heights since the 623 AIRs are split between them with segment 0 getting the taller traces.\n\nNow I need to focus on the optimization ideas rather than diving deeper into these dimensions.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":22707,"cache_read_input_tokens":104987,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":22707},"output_tokens":9,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f4b22a1a-5187-493b-801a-a22317879818","uuid":"094912df-dac3-4107-8f65-c885645a89da"}
