ully—it has a stacked matrix height of 2^19 with width 1, and the codeword height is 2^21 with log_blowup=2, giving 2M total elements. That's 4× smaller than seg 0's 48M. So the NTT is only 2M-point instead of 8M, bit reversal is ~1ms, and the Merkle tree with 131K leaves hashing 16 elements each should be very fast. Total GPU time for seg 1 is probably around 10ms max.\n\nBut the wall time is 87ms, leaving a 77ms gap that doesn't match the GPU work. I think what's happening is there's a synchronization point at the start of stacked_commit—the trace generation phase likely launched async GPU kernels that are still running when trace commit begins. The first kernel in trace commit (like filling zeros in stack_traces_into_expanded) would implicitly wait for those prior GPU operations to finish. So the \"trace commit\" wall time actually includes the tail end of trace generation GPU work that hadn't completed yet, not just the trace commit kernels themselves.\n\nTo verify this, I need to check if there's a synchronization before the timing span starts. Looking at the code, the span is entered right before calling `self.device.commit()`, so the timing includes whatever synchronization happens at that point.\n\nThe commit operation calls `stacked_commit`, which starts with a GPU memset operation on the default stream. If the prior trace generation work is also on the default stream, this memset will implicitly wait for that work to complete. So the 87ms trace commit timing is likely dominated by waiting for async trace gen to finish, with the actual GPU work being much smaller.\n\nThis means the 254ms trace commit at APC 300 is probably split between waiting for trace gen (~150-200ms) and actual commit work (~50-100ms), and since the trace gen is outside our scope, there's not much we can optimize here.\n\nBut wait—the drain-gpu-pipeline task from earlier actually resolved this. It added a sync point before STARK timing starts and found zero pending work, meaning all trace gen kernels complete before `prove()` is even called. So the 87ms stacked_commit for segment 1 really is just the actual trace commit work itself, not waiting on anything else.\n\nNow I'm digging into why the GPU kernel work is taking so much longer than expected. Without NVTX annotations on the trace commit span, I can't directly map the nsight kernels to that phase. Let me examine the `batch_ntt` function more carefully—the NTT operations might be taking longer than my initial estimate. Looking at the nsight data, the `_CT_NTT` kernels from the icicle library show 75 instances totaling 112ms with an average of 1.5ms each, which is significant. For the app proof with 2 segments, the NTT gets called during RS encoding, so that's likely a major contributor to the commit time. 2. `batch_ntt_small` (inverse NTT for l_skip): 2 calls\n3. `mle_interpolate_stages`: not NTT but subset-zeta transform (different kernel)\n\nPlus for WHIR and recursion, additional NTT calls.\n\nThe largest NTT instances (13.9ms) are likely from the recursion proofs (width 27, height 16M). For the app proof with width 6, height 8M (seg 0) or width 1, height 2M (seg 1), the NTT should be smaller.\n\nWith 6 columns of 8M points, the `batch_ntt` function processes all columns in a single batch call to the icicle `_CT_NTT` kernel. For 48M total points, I'm estimating around 384MB of memory bandwidth and roughly 1.1B multiply-adds, which should take about 1.5ms—though the icicle implementation's staged approach with multiple kernel launches between stages likely adds overhead. For 8M points specifically, the NTT probably splits into three stages: within-block butterflies, inter-block twiddle operations, and final transformations.\n\nAccounting for the bit reversal kernel, the small inverse NTT, and the subset-zeta transform, the forward NTT alone is probably around 6ms, with the full RS encoding pipeline hitting roughly 11ms for segment 0. The Merkle tree compression and stacking operations add an