eTree own the arena buffer directly and store per-layer offsets instead of individual DeviceBuffers—that handles the lifetime issue properly.\n\nBefore I lock this in though, I'm reconsidering whether this is actually the best path. The 20-50ms savings is decent but uncertain, and I should weigh it against other optimization candidates like pre-warming the memory pool (potentially 50-76ms with simpler implementation) or moving the stacked reduction to GPU (5-10ms but high confidence).\n\nPre-warming looks promising—I'd allocate and immediately free buffers of the exact sizes needed during initialization, so the pool has suitable blocks ready when stacked_commit runs later. The challenge is knowing those buffer sizes upfront, which depends on parameters like max_stacked_height and max_stacked_width that I need to calculate from the configuration.\n\nLooking at the actual numbers though, the maximum possible codeword buffer would be enormous—around 64GB—which far exceeds GPU memory. The issue is that w_stack is just an upper bound; the real stacked width varies per segment and depends on the trace data itself. So I can't pre-warm with theoretical maximums. Instead, I could pre-warm using the actual sizes from the previous segment once it completes, which would give me realistic buffer dimensions for the next iteration.\n\nBut this approach has limited impact. For APC 300 with only 2 segments, pre-warming helps segment 1, yet that segment already shows zero time for rs_code_matrix, suggesting the pool is already being reused effectively. So pre-warming doesn't gain much here.\n\nRather than trying to pre-allocate blindly, I should focus on the Merkle tree layers instead. The arena approach works well there because the total size is determined by query_stride, which is known upfront when the tree is constructed. Instead of allocating 19 separate buffers for digest layers, I could allocate one contiguous buffer and partition it internally.\n\nThe math is clean: with query_stride S, the total digests needed across all layers is 2S - 1. I'd allocate that as a single buffer, then create views for each layer using offset pointers. This reduces allocations from ~19 down to 1, and the implementation is straightforward—compute the total size, allocate once, slice into per-layer views, and pass the right pointers to compress operations.\n\nRefactoring the MerkleTreeGpu struct...\n\nEstimating the performance impact: eliminating ~18 allocations per tree saves roughly 3.6ms if each allocation costs 0.2ms. Across 12 trees in a typical APC 300 configuration, that's about 43ms total savings.\n\nBut I'm skeptical about that overhead estimate. With a warm pool, allocations should be nearly instant, so the real savings might be closer to negligible. I need to step back and find the optimization with the highest probability of delivering meaningful gains—at least 10-30ms—without excessive implementation complexity. Let me reassess the remaining bottlenecks to find the most impactful target.\n\nThe biggest untouched opportunity is LogUp GKR at 517ms, which accounts for 40% of STARK time excluding trace. Previous optimization attempts have focused on input evaluation, fractional sumcheck processing, and batching, but I haven't really dug into the outer structure—what happens between input evaluation and the fractional sumcheck, and what comes after. Looking at the per-segment breakdown, segment 0 shows LogUp GKR taking 349ms with input_evals at 237ms and fractional_sumcheck at 109ms, leaving only 3ms unaccounted for, while segment 1 is faster at 168ms with input_evals dropping to 62ms.\n\nThe fractional sumcheck is running about 5-5.5ms per inner round across both segments, and the compute kernel dominates the cost. Looking at the nsight profiling data, the compute_round_and_revert_kernel is averaging 0.5ms per instance across 141 calls, which suggests there's potential optimization in how these kernels are being invoked or structured.\n\nNow I'm examining where the remaining time goes—the measured fractional_sumcheck total is 213ms but my kernel accounting only gets to about 40ms, leaving a 173ms gap. Let me check the other GKR kernels like bit_rev_frac_build and multifold to see if they're contributing significantly to this overhead. Looking at the total GPU kernel time across all GKR operations—the core fraction tree kernels sum to 233ms, while the input evaluation kernels add another 515ms for a combined 748ms. The measured wall time is only 517ms though, which makes sense because the input evaluations run in parallel across 8 streams. The fractional sumcheck phase shows an interesting discrepancy: the GPU kernels total 233ms but the wall time is only 213ms, suggesting there's some overlap or efficiency gain happening there.\n\nI'm spending too much time on this analysis. Based on everything I've looked at, I'm going to focus on a concrete optimization: keeping the G evaluation buffers on the GPU and doing the NTT multiply-and-accumulate operations there for the stacked reduction in round-0 polynomial reconstruction.\n\nThe D2H synchronization stalls from the `to_host()` calls are adding significant overhead—roughly 30-50ms across the segments—which actually exceeds the 14ms that round-0 itself takes. These stalls force the GPU pipeline to drain and wait for prior work to complete, so moving that computation onto the GPU should help more than the cheap CPU-side NTT would suggest.\n\nActually, looking at the breakdown more carefully: the D2H sync is only about 2ms per segment when accounting for the actual kernel time, transfer, and CPU reconstruction overhead. That's just 4ms total savings across both segments, which is nowhere near the rollback threshold. The stacked reduction with l_skip=4 isn't worth optimizing.\n\nI need to step back and evaluate all the optimization candidates by their expected value—probability times impact—to pick the one that actually matters. Looking at the performance metrics, the arena Merkle tree layers and WHIR buffer reuse stand out as the most impactful optimizations with the best expected value returns. I'm noticing they're actually interconnected — both tackle allocation overhead in the Merkle tree construction that gets called repeatedly throughout the prover. The arena-based approach would consolidate per-layer DeviceBuffer allocations into a single operation, which should eliminate a significant chunk of CUDA memory overhead across every Merkle tree in the system. Before I finalize this as my optimization target, I should verify that per-layer allocations are actually a meaningful bottleneck rather than just assuming they are.\n\nLooking at the nsight kernel summary to understand the CPU overhead between kernel launches, I'm trying to estimate what portion of total wall time is spent on GPU kernels versus CPU overhead. The kernel summary shows time percentages for the major kernels like apc_apply_bus, evaluate_interactions_gkr, and the various round kernels, so I can add those up to see how much of the total execution time is actually spent computing on the GPU. I'm summing up all the kernel execution times from the nsight report to verify they add up to the total GPU time—the percentages should reach around 100%, and the absolute milliseconds across all kernels should match the overall GPU execution duration. Looking at the GPU utilization with multi-streaming, I realize the kernel time measurements get complicated when multiple threads are running concurrently. Rather than trying to extract precise overhead numbers from the profiler, I should focus on a broader optimization strategy. I'm thinking about a cross-cutting improvement: replacing all the `to_host()` calls with stream-ordered versions throughout the STARK prover, since the current implementation uses a global mutex that serializes device-to-host copies across threads. This could unlock better parallelism across the entire pipeline.\n\nThe MLE evaluation code already made this switch, but I should check whether other hot paths like stacked reduction, fractional sumcheck, round 0, trace commit, and WHIR still have the old `to_host()` calls that are acquiring that global mutex and creating bottlenecks.\n\nLooking at the contention more carefully though—most of these phases run single-threaded anyway, so the mutex isn't really a problem. The multi-threaded phases already got fixed or are using the stream-local version. This optimization probably won't move the needle much.\n\nI'm going to focus on something more concrete instead: consolidating the per-layer Merkle tree digest allocations into a single arena, and applying the same pattern to WHIR's per-round codeword buffers. This directly reduces memory pool churn across all the tree constructions in the prover and is straightforward to verify.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":11691,"cache_read_input_tokens":150678,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":11691},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f4b22a1a-5187-493b-801a-a22317879818","uuid":"6a81fb30-39ac-4ac1-9cc4-36becfdd59f2"}
