{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01HoKvxbDcw7CWYHfZ89bPNM","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Plan: Arena-based Merkle tree digest layer allocation\n2\t\n3\t## Goal\n4\t\n5\tReplace the per-layer `DeviceBuffer<D>::with_capacity()` allocations in `MerkleTreeGpu::new_generic_with_hash` (and `new_with_hash` for EF) with a single arena allocation. Currently, building a Merkle tree with `query_stride = S` makes `log2(S)` separate CUDA memory pool operations (one per tree layer). At APC 300, 12 Merkle trees are constructed per proof (2 trace commits + 10 WHIR rounds), producing ~228 separate `cudaMallocAsync` calls just for digest layers. The first-segment stacked_commit takes 163ms vs the second segment's 87ms despite similar GPU kernel work — the 76ms gap is allocation and pool overhead. This optimization reduces each tree's digest allocations from ~19 to 1, eliminating per-layer pool overhead and cold-start allocation penalties.\n6\t\n7\t## Current Code Path\n8\t\n9\t### Merkle tree construction (`crates/cuda-backend/src/merkle_tree.rs:113-172`)\n10\t\n11\t```\n12\tnew_generic_with_hash<MH>()\n13\t  1. query_stride = height / rows_per_query                         [line 126]\n14\t  2. DeviceBuffer::<D>::with_capacity(query_stride)  ← ALLOC 0      [line 127]\n15\t  3. MH::compress_rows(...)                                          [lines 130-137]\n16\t  4. LOOP while last_layer.len() > 1:                                [lines 143-159]\n17\t     a. DeviceBuffer::<D>::with_capacity(prev.len() / 2)  ← ALLOC N [line 145]\n18\t     b. MH::compress_layer(...)                                      [lines 152-157]\n19\t  5. d_root.to_host()  ← SYNC + D2H                                 [line 163]\n20\t  6. Return MerkleTreeGpu { digest_layers: Vec<DeviceBuffer<D>>, ... }\n21\t```\n22\t\n23\t**Allocation count**: 1 + log2(query_stride) per tree. For `query_stride = 2^19` (trace commit seg 0 at APC 300), that's 20 allocations.\n24\t\n25\t**Layer sizes** (Digest = `[F; 8]` = 32 bytes):\n26\t- Layer 0: `query_stride` digests (e.g., 524,288 × 32 = 16 MB)\n27\t- Layer 1: `query_stride / 2` (8 MB)\n28\t- Layer k: `query_stride / 2^k`\n29\t- Total: `2 * query_stride - 1` digests (e.g., ~32 MB)\n30\t\n31\t### How layers are used later (`batch_query_proofs`, lines 278-357)\n32\t\n33\tThe `batch_query_proofs` method accesses layers via:\n34\t```rust\n35\ttree.digest_layers.iter().take(depth).map(|layer| layer.as_ptr() as u64)\n36\t```\n37\tIt passes raw device pointers to the `query_digest_layers` CUDA kernel. The kernel indexes into each layer independently. **No assumption is made that layers are contiguous in memory.**\n38\t\n39\t### Extension field variant (`crates/cuda-backend/src/merkle_tree.rs:403-463`)\n40\t\n41\tIdentical allocation pattern using `DeviceBuffer::<D>::with_capacity()` per layer. Same optimization applies.\n42\t\n43\t## Changes\n44\t\n45\t### Change 1: Add arena constructor to `MerkleTreeGpu` (`merkle_tree.rs`)\n46\t\n47\tReplace the body of `new_generic_with_hash<MH>()` (lines 113-172):\n48\t\n49\t**Before** (current code): Allocates a separate `DeviceBuffer<D>` per layer.\n50\t\n51\t**After**: \n52\t1. Compute `total_digests = 2 * query_stride - 1` (geometric sum of all layers).\n53\t2. Allocate ONE `DeviceBuffer::<D>::with_capacity(total_digests)` — the arena.\n54\t3. Create non-owning `DeviceBuffer<D>` views into the arena for each layer using `DeviceBuffer::from_raw_parts_non_owning()` (existing API at `d_buffer.rs:73-80`).\n55\t4. Call `MH::compress_rows()` with a pointer into the arena's first region (offset 0, length `query_stride`).\n56\t5. Loop: call `MH::compress_layer()` with pointers into successive arena regions.\n57\t6. Extract root via `d_root.to_host()` on the last layer view.\n58\t7. Store `arena: DeviceBuffer<D>` (owning) + `digest_layers: Vec<DeviceBuffer<D>>` (non-owning views) + `rows_per_query` + `root` in the struct.\n59\t\n60\t**Key**: The `compress_rows` and `compress_layer` FFI functions take raw `*mut D` pointers (via `DeviceBuffer::as_mut_ptr()`). These work identically whether the buffer is owning or a non-owning view — only the pointer and length matter.\n61\t\n62\t### Change 2: Add `arena` field to `MerkleTreeGpu` struct (`merkle_tree.rs:52-64`)\n63\t\n64\t```rust\n65\tpub struct MerkleTreeGpu<F, Digest> {\n66\t    pub(crate) backing_matrix: Option<DeviceMatrix<F>>,\n67\t    pub(crate) arena: Option<DeviceBuffer<Digest>>,     // NEW: owns the digest memory\n68\t    pub(crate) digest_layers: Vec<DeviceBuffer<Digest>>, // now non-owning views\n69\t    pub(crate) rows_per_query: usize,\n70\t    pub(crate) root: Digest,\n71\t}\n72\t```\n73\t\n74\tThe `arena` is `Option` to handle degenerate cases (query_stride ≤ 1, where the tree is trivial and no arena is needed). When `arena` is `Some`, `digest_layers` contains non-owning views. When `None`, `digest_layers` contains owning buffers (fallback for edge cases).\n75\t\n76\t**Drop order**: Rust drops struct fields in declaration order. `arena` is declared before `digest_layers`, but the non-owning views in `digest_layers` are dropped first (Vec drop iterates elements). Since non-owning `DeviceBuffer` drop is a no-op (line 196: `if self.owns_memory && ...`), the arena is freed after all views are dropped. This is correct.\n77\t\n78\tWait — Rust drops fields in **declaration order**, meaning `backing_matrix` first, then `arena`, then `digest_layers`. This is WRONG — the non-owning views in `digest_layers` would point to freed arena memory during their drop. However, since non-owning drop is a no-op, it doesn't actually access the freed memory. Still, to be safe, declare `arena` AFTER `digest_layers`:\n79\t\n80\t```rust\n81\tpub struct MerkleTreeGpu<F, Digest> {\n82\t    pub(crate) backing_matrix: Option<DeviceMatrix<F>>,\n83\t    pub(crate) digest_layers: Vec<DeviceBuffer<Digest>>,  // non-owning views, dropped first (no-op)\n84\t    pub(crate) arena: Option<DeviceBuffer<Digest>>,        // owning, dropped after views\n85\t    pub(crate) rows_per_query: usize,\n86\t    pub(crate) root: Digest,\n87\t}\n88\t```\n89\t\n90\t### Change 3: Apply same arena pattern to EF Merkle tree (`merkle_tree.rs:403-463`)\n91\t\n92\tSame changes as Change 1, applied to `new_with_hash<MH>()` for `MerkleTreeGpu<EF, D>`.\n93\t\n94\t### Change 4: Ensure `batch_query_proofs` works with non-owning views (`merkle_tree.rs:278-357`)\n95\t\n96\t**No change needed.** The `batch_query_proofs` method accesses `layer.as_ptr()` (line 298), which returns the raw device pointer regardless of `owns_memory`. The CUDA kernel reads from these pointers. Non-owning views have valid pointers into the arena.\n97\t\n98\t## Invariants\n99\t\n100\t1. **Memory lifetime**: The arena buffer must outlive all non-owning layer views. Guaranteed by struct field drop order (digest_layers dropped before arena, and non-owning drop is a no-op).\n101\t2. **Pointer validity**: Each layer view points to a disjoint region within the arena. The offset computation is: `layer_k_offset = query_stride + query_stride/2 + ... + query_stride/2^(k-1) = 2*query_stride - query_stride/2^(k-1)`. Wait, simpler: `offset_0 = 0`, `offset_k = offset_{k-1} + size_{k-1}` where `size_k = query_stride / 2^k`.\n102\t3. **Total arena size**: `sum(query_stride / 2^k for k=0..num_layers-1) = query_stride * (2 - 2^(1-num_layers)) < 2 * query_stride`. Exact: `2 * query_stride - 1` digests.\n103\t4. **Correctness**: `compress_rows` and `compress_layer` write to output pointers passed as arguments. They don't assume output buffers are independently allocated. The output buffers must be non-overlapping (guaranteed by disjoint arena regions).\n104\t5. **APC 0 regression**: APC 0 has fewer, larger Merkle trees. The arena approach saves allocations there too but the per-allocation cost is lower (warm pool). No regression expected since the arena is always ≤ the sum of individual allocations.\n105\t6. **Root extraction**: `d_root.to_host()` reads from the arena's last region. This works identically to reading from an owning buffer.\n106\t7. **query_stride = 1 edge case**: When `query_stride = 1`, there are 0 tree layers (only the root). The arena has 1 element. The loop body never executes. This works correctly.\n107\t\n108\t## Measurement Plan\n109\t\n110\t### Commands\n111\t\n112\t```bash\n113\t# APC 300 (primary metric):\n114\tcd /home/georg/powdr && RUST_LOG=info target/release/powdr_openvm_riscv prove \\\n115\t  --artifact results/pairing/apc300.cbor --input 0 \\\n116\t  --metrics /home/georg/stark-backend/autoopt-results/2026-04-15-0800-arena-merkle-tree-digest-layers/results/after_apc300.json \\\n117\t  --recursion\n118\t\n119\t# APC 0 (regression check):\n120\tcd /home/georg/powdr && RUST_LOG=info target/release/powdr_openvm_riscv prove \\\n121\t  --artifact results/pairing/apc000.cbor --input 0 \\\n122\t  --metrics /home/georg/stark-backend/autoopt-results/2026-04-15-0800-arena-merkle-tree-digest-layers/results/after_apc000.json \\\n123\t  --recursion\n124\t\n125\t# Analyze:\n126\tpython3 /home/georg/spec.py <metrics_path> <experiment_name>\n127\t```\n128\t\n129\t### Expected behavior\n130\t\n131\t- **STARK excl trace (APC 300)**: ≥30ms improvement (from ~1298ms to ≤1268ms). The primary savings come from trace commit (12 Merkle trees × ~18 fewer allocations × allocation overhead).\n132\t- **stacked_commit_time_ms (seg 0)**: ≥20ms improvement (from 163ms). First-segment allocation overhead is the largest target.\n133\t- **prover.openings.whir_time_ms**: ≥5ms improvement per segment (from 50ms). WHIR builds 5 Merkle trees per segment.\n134\t- **APC 0**: No regression (≤ +10ms noise on STARK excl trace).\n135\t\n136\t### Rollback threshold\n137\t\n138\tRevert if STARK excl trace improvement at APC 300 is less than 10ms.\n139\t\n140\t## Rollback Criteria\n141\t\n142\t1. STARK excl trace at APC 300 improves by less than 10ms.\n143\t2. APC 0 STARK excl trace regresses by more than 20ms.\n144\t3. Any test failure in `cargo nextest run -p openvm-cuda-backend --test-threads=4`.\n145\t4. Any verification failure (invalid proofs).\n146\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01Gyq7DFJwFwKQtVPkm2Sa3z","session_id":"f4b22a1a-5187-493b-801a-a22317879818","uuid":"ab1c811c-9e1b-4168-bd6b-8084bc8a2712","timestamp":"2026-04-15T03:56:07.032Z"}
