{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01GVHuCjfidgVyXZitYTJyTp","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Plan: Pre-warm GPU memory pool at device initialization\n2\t\n3\t## Goal\n4\t\n5\tEliminate the cold-start allocation overhead in the first segment's `rs_code_matrix` by pre-warming the GPU memory pool (VPMM or cudaMallocAsync) during `GpuDevice::new()`. Per-segment metrics show `rs_code_matrix_time_ms` = 57ms for seg 0 vs 0ms for seg 1 at APC 300. The 57ms is wall time of the `rs_code_matrix()` function, which launches only asynchronous GPU kernels — the time comes from the first-time allocation of the 192MB codeword buffer through the memory pool. Segments 1+ get 0ms because the pool already has a suitable freed block. Pre-warming creates and frees a large buffer at device initialization, ensuring the pool has mapped pages ready for the first prove() call.\n6\t\n7\t## Current Code Path\n8\t\n9\t### Memory allocation flow (`crates/cuda-common/src/memory_manager/mod.rs:70-100`)\n10\t\n11\t```\n12\tDeviceBuffer::<T>::with_capacity(len)  [d_buffer.rs:94]\n13\t  → MEMORY_MANAGER.lock().allocate(len * sizeof(T))  [d_buffer.rs:97]\n14\t    → if size < pool.page_size:\n15\t        cudaMallocAsync(ptr, size, cudaStreamPerThread)  [mod.rs:77]\n16\t      else:\n17\t        pool.allocate(size)  [vm_pool.rs — VPMM: cuMemCreate + cuMemMap]  [mod.rs:89]\n18\t```\n19\t\n20\tFor the 192MB codeword buffer (`codeword_height × width × 4` bytes):\n21\t- If VPMM active: `pool.allocate()` maps new physical pages via CUDA driver API — ~50ms for 96 pages\n22\t- If VPMM fallback: `cudaMallocAsync` triggers real `cudaMalloc` to grow the pool — ~10-50ms\n23\t\n24\t### Where the 57ms occurs (`crates/cuda-backend/src/stacked_pcs.rs:182-279`)\n25\t\n26\t```\n27\trs_code_matrix()                    [stacked_pcs.rs:182, #[instrument(skip_all)]]\n28\t  let mut codewords = DeviceBuffer::<F>::with_capacity(codeword_height * width);  ← COLD ALLOC\n29\t  batch_expand_pad(...)              ← async kernel launch\n30\t  mle_interpolate_stages(...)        ← async kernel launch  \n31\t  bit_rev(...)                       ← async kernel launch\n32\t  batch_ntt(...)                     ← async kernel launches\n33\t  return DeviceMatrix::new(...)\n34\t```\n35\t\n36\tThe `#[instrument]` span measures wall time. All GPU kernel launches are async (return immediately). The 57ms is dominated by the cold `with_capacity` call. For seg 1, the pool has the freed block from seg 0 → instant allocation → 0ms.\n37\t\n38\t### GpuDevice initialization (`crates/cuda-backend/src/device.rs:28-43`)\n39\t\n40\t```rust\n41\tpub fn new(config: SystemParams) -> Self {\n42\t    ensure_device_ntt_twiddles_initialized();\n43\t    let prover_config = GpuProverConfig { ... };\n44\t    let id = get_device().unwrap() as u32;\n45\t    let sm_count = get_sm_count(id).expect(\"failed to get SM count\");\n46\t    Self { config, prover_config, id, sm_count }\n47\t}\n48\t```\n49\t\n50\tNo GPU memory pre-warming currently occurs.\n51\t\n52\t## Changes\n53\t\n54\t### Change 1: Add pool pre-warming to `GpuDevice::new()` (`device.rs:28-43`)\n55\t\n56\tAfter `ensure_device_ntt_twiddles_initialized()`, add a pre-warm allocation and immediate free:\n57\t\n58\t```rust\n59\tpub fn new(config: SystemParams) -> Self {\n60\t    ensure_device_ntt_twiddles_initialized();\n61\t    \n62\t    // Pre-warm the GPU memory pool by allocating and freeing a buffer sized to cover\n63\t    // the largest expected single allocation (RS codeword buffer).\n64\t    // At APC 300 the codeword buffer is ~192MB; at APC 0 it can be larger.\n65\t    // This maps physical pages in VPMM (or grows the cudaMallocAsync pool),\n66\t    // avoiding cold-start overhead in the first segment's rs_code_matrix.\n67\t    {\n68\t        use openvm_cuda_common::d_buffer::DeviceBuffer;\n69\t        let warmup_elems = Self::pool_warmup_elements(&config);\n70\t        if warmup_elems > 0 {\n71\t            let _warmup = DeviceBuffer::<u32>::with_capacity(warmup_elems);\n72\t            // _warmup dropped here → pages return to pool, ready for reuse\n73\t        }\n74\t    }\n75\t    \n76\t    let prover_config = GpuProverConfig { ... };\n77\t    // ... rest unchanged\n78\t}\n79\t```\n80\t\n81\t### Change 2: Add helper to compute warmup size (`device.rs`)\n82\t\n83\t```rust\n84\timpl GpuDevice {\n85\t    /// Compute pool warmup allocation size in u32 elements.\n86\t    /// Sized to cover the RS codeword buffer (the largest single allocation in stacked_commit).\n87\t    fn pool_warmup_elements(config: &SystemParams) -> usize {\n88\t        // Codeword buffer size = stacked_height * blowup * w_stack * sizeof(F)\n89\t        // But w_stack is the MAX allowed width, not the actual width. The actual width\n90\t        // at APC 300 is 1-6. We use a cap of 256MB to avoid over-allocating.\n91\t        let max_stacked_height = 1usize << config.log_stacked_height();\n92\t        let max_codeword_height = max_stacked_height << config.log_blowup;\n93\t        // Estimate actual width: use min(w_stack, 8) as a reasonable upper bound\n94\t        let est_width = config.w_stack.min(8);\n95\t        let codeword_elems = max_codeword_height.checked_mul(est_width).unwrap_or(0);\n96\t        // Cap at 256MB / sizeof(u32) = 64M elements to avoid excessive memory\n97\t        let max_elems = 256 * 1024 * 1024 / std::mem::size_of::<u32>();\n98\t        codeword_elems.min(max_elems)\n99\t    }\n100\t}\n101\t```\n102\t\n103\tWith `l_skip=4, n_stack=17, log_blowup=2`: `max_codeword_height = 2^23 = 8M`, `est_width = min(2048, 8) = 8`. Warmup = 8M × 8 = 64M elements = 256MB. Capped at 256MB.\n104\t\n105\tAt APC 300 seg 0: actual codeword = 8M × 6 = 48M elements = 192MB. Fits within 256MB warmup.\n106\tAt APC 0 seg 0: actual codeword = 8M × ? (depends on stacking width). If > 256MB, the cap prevents over-allocation and the first segment still gets a partial warmup.\n107\t\n108\t### Why this works\n109\t\n110\tThe memory pool (VPMM or cudaMallocAsync) keeps freed memory available for reuse:\n111\t- **VPMM path**: Physical pages are mapped via `cuMemCreate` + `cuMemMap` during warmup. On free, pages return to the pool's free list (still mapped). Next allocation reuses mapped pages without driver API calls.\n112\t- **cudaMallocAsync path**: The async pool grows via real `cudaMalloc` during warmup. On free, memory stays in the pool. Next allocation finds a suitable block immediately.\n113\t\n114\tIn both cases, the warmup allocation + free takes ~50ms during `GpuDevice::new()` (outside any timing span), and subsequent allocations of ≤256MB are instant.\n115\t\n116\t## Invariants\n117\t\n118\t1. **No behavioral change**: The warmup allocates and immediately frees — no lasting side effect except warmed pool state.\n119\t2. **Memory safety**: The warmup buffer is owned by a local scope and dropped deterministically.\n120\t3. **No peak memory increase**: The warmup buffer is freed before any other allocation occurs.\n121\t4. **APC 0 compatibility**: The cap of 256MB limits over-allocation. APC 0 may allocate larger codeword buffers (if stacking width > 8), in which case the warmup provides partial benefit. No regression.\n122\t5. **VPMM not available**: If VPMM initialization fails (page_size = usize::MAX), all allocations go through cudaMallocAsync. The warmup still helps by growing the cudaMallocAsync pool.\n123\t\n124\t## Measurement Plan\n125\t\n126\t### Commands\n127\t\n128\t```bash\n129\t# Build (ensure binary is up to date):\n130\tcd /home/georg/powdr && cargo build --bin powdr_openvm_riscv -r --features \"metrics,cuda\"\n131\t\n132\t# APC 300 (primary metric):\n133\tcd /home/georg/powdr && RUST_LOG=info target/release/powdr_openvm_riscv prove \\\n134\t  --artifact results/pairing/apc300.cbor --input 0 \\\n135\t  --metrics <results_dir>/after_apc300.json --recursion\n136\t\n137\t# APC 0 (regression check):\n138\tcd /home/georg/powdr && RUST_LOG=info target/release/powdr_openvm_riscv prove \\\n139\t  --artifact results/pairing/apc000.cbor --input 0 \\\n140\t  --metrics <results_dir>/after_apc000.json --recursion\n141\t\n142\t# Analyze:\n143\tpython3 /home/georg/spec.py <metrics_path> <experiment_name>\n144\t```\n145\t\n146\t### Expected behavior\n147\t\n148\t- **rs_code_matrix_time_ms (seg 0)**: drops from 57ms to ≤5ms\n149\t- **stacked_commit_time_ms (seg 0)**: drops from 163ms to ~110ms\n150\t- **STARK excl trace (APC 300)**: ≥30ms improvement (from ~1298ms to ≤1268ms)\n151\t- **APC 0**: ≥20ms improvement (seg 0 rs_code_matrix was 47ms)\n152\t- **No regression at APC 0**: ≤ +10ms noise on STARK excl trace\n153\t\n154\t### Key metrics to check\n155\t\n156\t- `rs_code_matrix_time_ms` for seg 0 (should match seg 1's ~0ms)\n157\t- `stacked_commit_time_ms` for seg 0 vs seg 1 gap (should shrink from 76ms to ~20ms)\n158\t- Total `STARK excl trace` at APC 300 and APC 0\n159\t\n160\t## Rollback Criteria\n161\t\n162\t1. `STARK excl trace` improvement at APC 300 is less than 10ms.\n163\t2. APC 0 `STARK excl trace` regresses by more than 20ms.\n164\t3. Any test failure in `cargo nextest run -p openvm-cuda-backend --test-threads=4`.\n165\t4. Any verification failure (invalid proofs).\n166\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01WVbdLZJeweczgbWQ8Htego","session_id":"f4b22a1a-5187-493b-801a-a22317879818","uuid":"56fc0b0d-d00f-4d2e-9a3c-ffb0509de316","timestamp":"2026-04-15T04:14:14.606Z"}
